\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Component visible for the framework uses a CRTP template class $Component<>$. Interfaces through which the component is exposed as a dependency shall be abstract classes and are declared within that template with $as<>$ traits. Usual inheritance is also possible - the framework is not aware of it. In this example the component is exposed through $ExposedInterface1$ and $ExposedInterface2$ interfaces, while it simply inherits after the $OtherClass$ - without any side--effects. TODO CONSTRUCTOR}}{9}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:component_implementation}{{1}{9}{Component visible for the framework uses a CRTP template class $Component<>$. Interfaces through which the component is exposed as a dependency shall be abstract classes and are declared within that template with $as<>$ traits. Usual inheritance is also possible - the framework is not aware of it. In this example the component is exposed through $ExposedInterface1$ and $ExposedInterface2$ interfaces, while it simply inherits after the $OtherClass$ - without any side--effects. TODO CONSTRUCTOR}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Component visible for the framework uses a CRTP template class $Component<>$. Interfaces through which the component is exposed as a dependency shall be abstract classes and are declared within that template with $as<>$ traits. Usual inheritance is also possible - the framework is not aware of it. In this example the component is exposed through $MyFirstInterface$ and $MySecondInterface$ interfaces, while it simply inherits after the $OtherClass$ - without any side--effects.}}{9}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:aaa}{{2}{9}{Component visible for the framework uses a CRTP template class $Component<>$. Interfaces through which the component is exposed as a dependency shall be abstract classes and are declared within that template with $as<>$ traits. Usual inheritance is also possible - the framework is not aware of it. In this example the component is exposed through $MyFirstInterface$ and $MySecondInterface$ interfaces, while it simply inherits after the $OtherClass$ - without any side--effects}{figure.2}{}}
